      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00243:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Preparation of Acid Anhydrides"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00001",
          "segmentId": "m00243:para:para-00001"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00001",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00243:alt:fig-00001-alt",
              "text": "Benzoyl chloride reacts with sodium acetate in ether, forming acetic benzoic anhydride. In the product structure, a benzoyl group and an acetyl group are single-bonded to an oxygen atom."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_05_001.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00002",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00243:title:sect-00002-title",
        "text": "Reactions of Acid Anhydrides"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00002",
          "segmentId": "m00243:para:para-00002"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00002",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00243:alt:fig-00002-alt",
              "text": "The reaction scheme shows conversion of an acid anhydride to a carboxylic acid using water (hydrolysis), ester using alcohol (alcoholysis), amide using ammonia (aminolysis), aldehyde and primary alcohol using hydride ions (reduction)."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_05_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00003",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00243:title:sect-00003-title",
            "text": "Conversion of Acid Anhydrides into Esters"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00003",
              "segmentId": "m00243:para:para-00003"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00003",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00243:alt:fig-00003-alt",
                  "text": "Salicylic acid reacts with acetic anhydride in aqueous sodium hydroxide, giving aspirin and acetate ion. The aspirin structure comprises of a benzene ring with carboxylic acid and methyl acetate groups attached ortho to each other."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_05_003.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00004",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00243:title:sect-00004-title",
            "text": "Conversion of Acid Anhydrides into Amides"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00004",
              "segmentId": "m00243:para:para-00004"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00004",
              "class": "unnumbered",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00243:alt:fig-00004-alt",
                  "text": "The reaction of p-hydroxyaniline and acetic anhydride in the presence of aqueous sodium hydroxide forms an acetate ion and acetaminophen, where the para position of phenol is attached to an acetamide group."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_05_004.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
